const lessons = [
{slug:'audience', title:'Start with the reader',group:'Foundations',intro:'Technical writing helps someone understand a subject or complete a task. Start by deciding who that someone is.',body:`<p>Technical writing communicates specialized knowledge in a usable form. It appears in software guides, assembly instructions, procedures, proposals, and reports. The subject can be engineering, business, the arts, or any field with specialized methods.</p><h3>Keep the language clear. Adjust the detail.</h3><p>A first-time user and a repair technician need different information about the same product. Use familiar words for both. Change the depth, prerequisites, and examples to match their work.</p><div class="comparison"><div><span class="label">For a new user</span><p>Connect the printer to the same Wi-Fi network as your laptop.</p></div><div><span class="label">For a technician</span><p>Check the printer’s network configuration and confirm that its IP address is reachable from the client subnet.</p></div></div><h3>Before you draft</h3><ol><li>Name the reader and the task.</li><li>List what the reader already knows.</li><li>Identify what they need next.</li><li>Choose a format that makes that information easy to find.</li></ol>`,practice:'A new teammate needs to run your project locally. Write down what they know, what they need installed, and what a successful first run looks like.',answer:'For a teammate who knows Git but has never used this project: list the required runtime version, how to install dependencies, how to start the app, and the page or message they should see. Explain project-specific terms.',takeaway:'Match the information to the reader’s task, while keeping the language clear.'},
{slug:'definitions',title:'Build a useful definition',group:'Foundations',intro:'A good definition tells the reader what something is, where it belongs, and what makes it distinct.',body:`<div class="formula"><span>Term</span><b>+</b><span>Class</span><b>+</b><span>Distinguishing detail</span></div><h3>Give each part a job</h3><p>The <strong>term</strong> names the thing. The <strong>class</strong> places it in a recognizable group. The <strong>distinguishing detail</strong> separates it from other members of that group.</p><div class="annotated"><p><mark>A password manager</mark> is <mark>an application</mark> that <mark>stores login credentials and helps you retrieve them securely.</mark></p><div class="label">term / class / distinguishing detail</div></div><h3>Choose the narrowest useful class</h3><p>“Application” gives the reader more information than “thing.” A useful category should be familiar and accurate. Avoid defining a word with itself: “A compiler is something that compiles” offers little help.</p><p>Add distinguishing details that matter to the reader. A long list of incidental features makes the definition harder to use.</p>`,practice:'Define a backup in one sentence. Include the term, a class, and a detail that explains its purpose.',answer:'A backup is a copy of data kept so it can be restored if the original is lost or damaged. “Copy of data” is the class; the restoration purpose distinguishes it from an arbitrary duplicate.',takeaway:'Define with a recognizable category and a meaningful difference.'},
{slug:'categories',title:'Choose the right category',group:'Foundations',intro:'Class words give readers a starting point. Choose one that explains the object without forcing it into an artificial box.',body:`<p>The transcript introduces physical and digital class words. Treat these as useful vocabulary, rather than mutually exclusive scientific categories. A pair of scissors can be both a tool and a mechanism.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Useful class words, scroll horizontally if needed"><table><caption>Useful class words</caption><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>What it helps describe</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tool</td><td>An object used to do work</td><td>Screwdriver</td></tr><tr><td>Instrument</td><td>An object used for measurement, observation, or a specialized activity</td><td>Thermometer</td></tr><tr><td>Apparatus</td><td>Equipment or a setup used for a purpose</td><td>Laboratory distillation setup</td></tr><tr><td>Machine</td><td>A system of parts that performs work</td><td>Sewing machine</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>An arrangement that transmits or controls motion</td><td>Lock mechanism</td></tr><tr><td>Engine</td><td>A machine that converts energy into mechanical work</td><td>Combustion engine</td></tr><tr><td>Appliance</td><td>Equipment designed for a particular household or similar task</td><td>Microwave oven</td></tr><tr><td>Gadget</td><td>An informal name for a small, useful device</td><td>Fitness tracker</td></tr><tr><td>Device</td><td>A broad term that benefits from a qualifier</td><td>Electronic calculator</td></tr><tr><td>Hardware</td><td>Physical computer components</td><td>Keyboard</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Programs and related instructions</td><td>Operating system</td></tr><tr><td>Application</td><td>Software designed for a task</td><td>Photo editor</td></tr><tr><td>Platform</td><td>An environment that supports services or applications</td><td>Learning platform</td></tr><tr><td>Cloud service</td><td>Computing resources accessed over a network</td><td>Hosted storage service</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Categories overlap and meanings depend on the field. Machines do not all need gears, and cloud computing includes more than storage. Verify the definition for your actual subject.</p>`,practice:'Choose a useful class for a code editor. Then explain why “software” alone might be too broad for a beginner.',answer:'A code editor is an application for writing and editing source code. “Software” is accurate, but “application” plus its purpose gives a beginner a more useful picture.',takeaway:'Use categories to help comprehension, and check where their boundaries overlap.'},
{slug:'concrete',title:'Make the meaning concrete',group:'Foundations',intro:'A one-sentence definition is a starting point. Add the detail your reader needs to picture it or tell it apart.',body:`<h3>Seven ways to expand a definition</h3><ol class="expanded-list"><li><strong>Assign a class.</strong> A barometer is an instrument for measuring atmospheric pressure.</li><li><strong>Describe its form.</strong> Explain shape, size, or arrangement when appearance matters.</li><li><strong>Explain the word’s origin.</strong> Use a verified root word if it helps the meaning stick.</li><li><strong>Move from general to specific.</strong> Define the broad concept, then introduce relevant types.</li><li><strong>Offer familiar wording.</strong> Explain a specialist term in everyday language without losing precision.</li><li><strong>Compare and contrast.</strong> Explain a shared purpose and a useful difference.</li><li><strong>Give an example.</strong> Show one recognizable instance of the concept.</li></ol><div class="example-block"><span class="label">Example: compare and contrast</span><p>A local backup stays on a device you control. A cloud backup stores a copy with a remote service. Both support recovery, but their access and failure conditions differ.</p></div><p>Choose the methods that answer a real question. You do not need all seven in every definition.</p>`,practice:'Explain a browser cache using a definition, familiar wording, and one concrete example.',answer:'A browser cache is local storage for copies of website resources. It helps the browser reuse files it has already downloaded. For example, it may reuse a site’s logo instead of downloading it again on every page.',takeaway:'Expand a definition with examples and distinctions that help this reader.'},
{slug:'objects',title:'Describe an object',group:'Explain and instruct',intro:'Help the reader recognize the object, understand its parts, and see how those parts work together.',body:`<h3>Use four layers</h3><ol><li><strong>Identity and purpose.</strong> Define the object and say what it does.</li><li><strong>Appearance.</strong> Show its shape and arrangement with a labeled diagram when useful.</li><li><strong>Components.</strong> Describe the relevant parts in a consistent order.</li><li><strong>Operation.</strong> Explain how the parts work together.</li></ol><figure class="object-diagram"><svg role="img" aria-labelledby="usb-title usb-desc" viewBox="0 0 660 240"><title id="usb-title">A simplified USB flash drive</title><desc id="usb-desc">A connector on the left attaches to a body on the right. The body contains the controller and memory chip.</desc><rect x="50" y="85" width="130" height="76" fill="#bbbdb2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="180" y="65" width="350" height="116" rx="5" fill="#d7e1ca" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="225" y="98" width="70" height="50" fill="#354b43"/><rect x="365" y="98" width="110" height="50" fill="#354b43"/><path d="M110 85V34H55 M260 148V209H185 M420 98V34H514" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/><g fill="currentColor" font-family="DM Mono,monospace" font-size="13"><text x="25" y="25">1. connector</text><text x="130" y="230">2. controller</text><text x="480" y="25">3. memory</text></g></svg><figcaption>Illustrative diagram: the same part names appear in the description.</figcaption></figure><p><strong>A USB flash drive</strong> is a portable storage device. Its connector links it to a compatible port. The controller manages transfers between the computer and the memory chip, where data is stored.</p><h3>Keep the description easy to follow</h3><p>Use the same labels in the diagram and text. Choose an order—such as left to right or input to output—and stay with it. Include dimensions, materials, or specifications only when relevant and verified.</p>`,practice:'Describe a keyboard in four short parts: purpose, appearance, components, and operation.',answer:'Purpose: enter text and commands. Appearance: a board of labeled keys. Components: keys, switches or sensors, a controller, and a connection. Operation: pressing a key sends an input signal to the connected device. Adapt the details to the keyboard model.',takeaway:'Describe purpose, appearance, parts, and operation in a consistent order.'},
{slug:'notices',title:'Put notices before the risk',group:'Explain and instruct',intro:'A useful notice explains what could happen and what the reader should do before they reach that point.',body:`<p>Notices call attention to information that affects a task. Place each notice beside the relevant step, before the reader encounters the hazard or consequence.</p><div class="notice note"><span class="label">Note</span><p>Add helpful context: “Large exports may take several minutes.”</p></div><div class="notice caution"><span class="label">Caution · illustrative data example</span><p>Exporting to an existing filename may replace your earlier file. Choose a new filename to keep both versions.</p></div><h3>Write the consequence and the action</h3><p>“Be careful” is too vague. Name the specific issue and the step that avoids it. Keep the notice short enough to read at the point of action.</p><h3>Use the applicable notice system</h3><p>The transcript teaches note, caution, warning, and danger with colored boxes. Those definitions and colors are a course convention, not a universal safety standard. For equipment or safety documentation, use the applicable standard and manufacturer’s guidance.</p><p>Keep a visible text label. Color alone should never carry the notice’s meaning. Place essential notices in the main flow rather than hiding them in a collapsed panel.</p>`,practice:'Write a notice for a step that overwrites an existing configuration file. Include the consequence and a way to preserve the old version.',answer:'Caution: This step replaces config.json. Save a copy of your current config.json before continuing if you need to keep its settings. The notice belongs before the replacement step.',takeaway:'Place a specific consequence and preventive action before the relevant step.'},
{slug:'instructions',title:'Write steps people can follow',group:'Explain and instruct',intro:'Give readers a clear starting point, one useful action at a time, and a way to recognize the result.',body:`<p>A manual may include product background, operating instructions, notices, troubleshooting, and support information. A single procedure should focus on a specific task.</p><h3>Start each step with an action</h3><div class="comparison"><div><span class="label">Indirect</span><p>The file is selected by the operator, who then initiates the export process.</p></div><div><span class="label">Imperative</span><p>Select the file. Choose Export.</p></div></div><p>The imperative is the command form of a verb: select, open, connect, save. It speaks directly to the reader without repeating “you” in every sentence.</p><div class="procedure"><span class="label">Illustrative procedure · export a document</span><p><strong>Before you start:</strong> Open the document in an editor that supports PDF export.</p><ol><li>Open the File menu.</li><li>Choose Export, then select PDF.</li><li>Choose a folder and enter a filename.</li><li>Select Save.</li><li>Open the PDF and check that its pages display correctly.</li></ol></div><h3>Check the sequence</h3><p>Use numbered steps for order, descriptive subheadings for phases, and labeled images when they clarify an action. Match menu labels to the real product. State prerequisites and place notices before consequential actions.</p>`,practice:'Rewrite: “The user should now proceed to the settings area, where the notification preferences can be modified.”',answer:'Open Settings. Select Notifications. Choose the notifications you want to receive. Add the exact save action if this interface requires one.',takeaway:'Lead with verbs, follow the task’s order, and make the result observable.'},
{slug:'processes',title:'Explain how a process works',group:'Explain and instruct',intro:'Make the starting conditions, stages, and result visible. Decide whether you are explaining the process or directing someone through it.',body:`<p>A process description explains what happens and why. A procedure tells someone what to do. The transcript combines these approaches; choose the one that fits the reader’s purpose.</p><h3>Give the process a clear boundary</h3><ol><li>Define its purpose and outcome.</li><li>List inputs, materials, or prerequisites.</li><li>Describe the stages in order.</li><li>Explain important changes between stages.</li><li>State the final result and relevant checks.</li></ol><div class="process-flow"><div><span class="label">Input</span><p>Source document</p></div><div><span class="label">Transformation</span><p>PDF export</p></div><div><span class="label">Output</span><p>Shareable PDF</p></div></div><div class="example-block"><span class="label">Description</span><p>During export, the editor converts the document’s content and layout into a PDF. The resulting file can be opened in a PDF reader.</p><span class="label">Instruction</span><p>Choose Export, select PDF, and save the file.</p></div><p>For a hands-on process, include the materials, numbered actions, diagrams, and notices needed to carry it out. Follow equipment-specific guidance rather than treating a generic description as an operating manual.</p>`,practice:'Write one sentence explaining what happens during a software build, then one sentence telling a developer how to start this project’s build.',answer:'Description: The build process transforms the project’s source files into distributable output. Instruction: Run the documented build command from the project folder. Replace that generic phrase with the verified command for the actual project.',takeaway:'Separate what happens from what the reader must do.'},
{slug:'report-types',title:'Choose a report’s purpose',group:'Reports',intro:'Decide whether the report needs to present information, interpret findings, or recommend an action.',body:`<p>A report organizes information for a reader and a purpose. It may use original observations, existing sources, or both. Cite the sources and distinguish evidence from interpretation.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Report categories, scroll horizontally if needed"><table><caption>The transcript’s functional categories</caption><thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>What it includes</th><th>Illustrative example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Informational</td><td>Facts</td><td>Five delivery businesses operate in this area.</td></tr><tr><td>Examinational</td><td>Facts and interpretation</td><td>Five businesses operate here; the available financial data suggests limited profitability.</td></tr><tr><td>Analytical</td><td>Facts, interpretation, and a recommendation</td><td>Compare demand and costs in another area before deciding where to expand.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>“Examinational” is the transcript’s label. Terminology varies across organizations; analytical reports do not always require a recommendation.</p><h3>Other useful ways to classify reports</h3><ul><li><strong>Subject:</strong> engineering, finance, research, or another field.</li><li><strong>Frequency:</strong> recurring or prepared for a particular assignment.</li><li><strong>Formality:</strong> a brief internal update or a formal submission.</li><li><strong>Format:</strong> memo, letter, short report, or longer document.</li><li><strong>Relationship:</strong> internal, prepared by an outside specialist, or intended for a public audience.</li></ul><p>The required format depends on your organization, assignment, and readers. Confirm those constraints before drafting.</p>`,practice:'A manager asks which hosting option the team should choose. What must the report include beyond a list of prices?',answer:'Compare the options against the team’s requirements, explain the evidence and tradeoffs, and make a recommendation with its assumptions and limits. Price alone does not answer which option fits.',takeaway:'Let the reader’s decision determine the report’s evidence and structure.'},
{slug:'report-structure',title:'Build a report people can use',group:'Reports',intro:'Give readers a short route to the answer and enough supporting detail to assess how you reached it.',body:`<div class="report-stack"><div><span class="label">Front matter</span><p>Title, contents, lists of figures or tables, executive summary</p></div><div><span class="label">Main body</span><p>Introduction, context, methods, findings, discussion, conclusions</p></div><div><span class="label">Back matter</span><p>References, appendices, supporting material</p></div></div><h3>Set up the main body</h3><p>Introduce the purpose, scope, background, and limitations. Explain how the information was collected. Present the findings, then show how they support the conclusions. Add recommendations when the task calls for them.</p><p>For a survey, describe the participants, recruitment, questions, collection period, and analysis. Explain sampling limits. A large response count alone does not establish a representative sample or prove a hypothesis.</p><h3>Make evidence traceable</h3><p>Use descriptive headings that match the table of contents. Label tables and figures consistently and explain what each shows. Include sources and enough method detail to help readers judge the findings.</p><h3>Write the executive summary last</h3><p>Answer three questions: Why was the report prepared? How was the work done? What did it find? Include the main recommendation if one is required. The executive summary covers the report as a whole; the closing summary revisits the findings and conclusions.</p><div class="example-block"><span class="label">Illustrative executive summary</span><p>This report compares two hosting options for the documentation site. The comparison uses published pricing, a test deployment, and the team’s maintenance requirements. Both options support the current site; option A is recommended for its simpler deployment process. The comparison excludes future traffic growth and paid support.</p></div><p>Front and back matter help readers navigate and verify a report. Their exact order, pagination, summary length, and required headings depend on the assignment or house style.</p>`,practice:'Draft a three-sentence executive summary for a report you might write: purpose, method, and finding. Keep any recommendation tied to evidence.',answer:'Example: This report investigates slow local setup for new contributors. It reviews the setup guide and records three observed onboarding sessions. Missing prerequisites caused repeated failures, so the report recommends adding a tested prerequisite checklist. These are illustrative findings, not research conducted for this site.',takeaway:'Lead with the answer, then make the evidence and its limits easy to inspect.'}
];
